From b1c775a23617ee179d257c748a7eee5109716174 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ayiejosh <132535860+ayiejosh@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 23:16:52 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 218/269] gallium/pvr: render submission that mirrors the
 reference's, and what the GPU did with it

Ran the assembled draw on the real open kernel driver (desktop down, one 512x512 draw, board
restored to vendor afterwards; no faults). The jobs are accepted; they never complete - and neither
does a render whose control stream is nothing but STREAM_TERMINATE, so this is submission setup, not
draw state. The probe now measures that directly instead of only checking acceptance.

Fixes found by the hardware run, all silent-failure shaped:

- INDEX_LIST never set the primitive topology. Zero there is POINT_LIST, so triangle triples were
  binned as points. It now takes a topology parameter and the probe's control stream reproduces the
  captured draw's INDEX_LIST0 word exactly (0x68000600) - also non-indexed like the capture, with
  the address/instance packets present only when they carry something.
- The geometry firmware stream was being built into the same BO as the control stream it names, so
  the CR_VDM_CTRL_STREAM_BASE pointer named a buffer whose contents had just been overwritten. It
  now goes in its own buffer, with the capture's completeonterminate and usc_common_size values
  (TE_PSG bit 17, PDS_STATE0 word 0x20010000).
- Geometry and partial-render/fragment jobs are different types, so the kernel does not order them
  against each other. The reference chains them with syncobjs and submits THREE jobs (geometry,
  PARTIAL_RENDER fragment, fragment); pvr_job_submit_render is now that shape, and waits for the
  fragment job's completion syncobj on the CPU instead of reading the target straight after submit.
- New pvr_job_submit_geom_sync()/probe liveness check: prove whether the firmware completes a
  submission at all before attributing a blank target to draw state. It does not, for us.

Verification: probe still exits 0, ALL PASS under the DRM noop shim; on the real driver every
SUBMIT_JOBS is accepted and the geometry/fragment jobs do not signal within 2 s. The working Vulkan
driver renders through the same kernel and GPU at the same time (vkrender PASS), so this is our
context/HWRT/firmware-stream setup, not the hardware. Next: capture the reference's firmware streams
and context state and diff them field by field against ours; the control-stream side is already
capture-conformant.
